> Fixed centers: softness and distant-point ambiguity

For two fixed centers 0 and 10 and m=2, the left membership is

$$
u_{\rm left}(x)=\frac{(x-10)^2}{x^2+(x-10)^2}.
$$
\alttext(For fixed centers zero and ten, left membership is squared distance to ten divided by the sum of squared distances to both centers.)

It equals 1 at 0, 0 at 10 and 1/2 at 5. But as x tends to either infinity it returns
to 1/2, because both distances become almost equal. A distant outlier can therefore
look maximally ambiguous rather than receive low membership in every cluster.
The original report's smooth transition describes its central region; globally
this function is not a monotone S-shaped curve.

For fixed positive distances, m approaching 1 concentrates membership on the
unique nearest center; m increasing without bound approaches uniform membership.
Those are fixed-center limits, not promises about the solutions of a refitted
floating-point optimizer. A value too close to 1 can overflow ratio powers; huge
m can underflow every powered weight in a center update. The native API reports
these numerical failures rather than presenting them as a successful fit.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fcm_softness.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/fuzzy_c_means.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const dm::algorithms::FuzzyCMeansMatrix centers{{0},{10}};
  dm::algorithms::FuzzyCMeansMatrix query;std::vector<double>x;
  for(int position=-40;position<=50;++position){query.push_back({double(position)});x.push_back(position);}
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const std::array<double,4> m{1.5,2.,3.,5.};
  const std::array<dm::graphics::RGB,4> colors{{{37,99,235},{220,38,38},{22,163,74},{147,51,234}}};
  std::print("| m | u_left(0) | u_left(5) | u_left(10) | u_left(-1000000) | u_left(1000000) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t k=0;k<m.size();++k){
    const auto u=dm::algorithms::fuzzy_c_means_membership(query,centers,m[k]);std::vector<double>y;for(const auto& row:u)y.push_back(row[0]);plot.lines(x,y,std::format("m={}",m[k]),colors[k],2);
    const auto check=dm::algorithms::fuzzy_c_means_membership({{0},{5},{10},{-1e6},{1e6}},centers,m[k]);
    std::print("| {:.1f} | {:.1f} | {:.1f} | {:.1f} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} |\n",m[k],check[0][0],check[1][0],check[2][0],check[3][0],check[4][0]);
  }
  plot.legend({"m=1.5","m=2","m=3","m=5"},{{37,99,235},{220,38,38},{22,163,74},{147,51,234}})
   .abline_h(.5,{120,120,120},1).abline_v(0,{120,120,120},1).abline_v(10,{120,120,120},1)
   .title("Fixed centers 0 and 10; both far tails tend to 1/2").xlab("Query x; far outside the central region is also ambiguous").ylab("Membership in center 0").y_limits(0,1.05).size(1100,540).save_svg("build/fcm_softness.svg");
}
